the interview was conducted via AM radio from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:48.840 --> 01:48:55.560
a separate observation chamber as the biological&nbsp;
transmitter seemed to be scp-22 73's only method&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:55.560 --> 01:49:02.100
of communication the interview began with Dr&nbsp;
Friedrich speaking to scp-2273 in Russian only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:02.100 --> 01:49:08.700
for 2273 to respond that Dr friedrich's accent was&nbsp;
atrocious and requesting he used German instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:08.700 --> 01:49:16.260
scp-2273 who according to the organic military&nbsp;
patches on his body was named Major Alexi belotrov&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:16.260 --> 01:49:21.900
initially resisted being interviewed he believed&nbsp;
that he was a prisoner of war being held by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:21.900 --> 01:49:26.760
American Military according to him he had been&nbsp;
in the middle of battle and that the Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:26.760 --> 01:49:31.500
had killed his men and left him for dead in&nbsp;
the wilderness nothing that Dr Friedrich told&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:31.500 --> 01:49:36.960
him that there was no war or that the foundation&nbsp;
was not affiliated with the U.S government could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:36.960 --> 01:49:42.180
convince him otherwise and he refused to talk&nbsp;
further before a second interview was conducted&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:42.180 --> 01:49:48.000
velotrov was given an old copy of the level one&nbsp;
researcher General debrief as a way of helping him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:48.000 --> 01:49:53.280
further understand the nature of the foundation&nbsp;
and his containment belatrave was more Cooperative&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:53.280 --> 01:49:58.080
after that though the realization that he would&nbsp;
most likely be kept in containment for the rest&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.080 --> 01:50:03.120
of his life caused him great distress and he was&nbsp;
unwilling to speak to Dr Friedrich for another&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:03.120 --> 01:50:09.360
three days Dr Friedrich didn't give up though and&nbsp;
when belatrop finally opened up to the doctor he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:09.360 --> 01:50:15.420
began to tell him more and more bellatrop told the&nbsp;
doctor that when he was a child Russia was hit by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:15.420 --> 01:50:21.300
an American nuclear warhead and that the Soviet&nbsp;
Union immediately retaliated the resulting nuclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:21.300 --> 01:50:27.720
conflict which belatrop called the war to end the&nbsp;
world caused massive Devastation and left most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.720 --> 01:50:33.900
the planet dangerously irradiated that is why he&nbsp;
had the exoskeleton it was to Shield him from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:33.900 --> 01:50:39.840
radiation and allow him to survive above ground&nbsp;
unfortunately velotrov knew very little about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:39.840 --> 01:50:45.300
how the suit was made or how it worked saying&nbsp;
that it was built by the engineers and that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:45.300 --> 01:50:51.840
grew naturally over a period of several years but&nbsp;
bellatrov hadn't made the decision himself it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:51.840 --> 01:50:56.580
his parents who had volunteered him as a test&nbsp;
subject for the exoskeleton suit program when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:56.580 --> 01:51:01.740
he was still young hoping it would give him a&nbsp;
better chance at survival the process of bonding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:01.740 --> 01:51:07.560
the exoskeleton to the human body was extremely&nbsp;
painful but according to balatrov its advantages&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:07.560 --> 01:51:13.680
far outweighed the pain the suit not only helped&nbsp;
him survive in a nuclear Wasteland but made him&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:13.680 --> 01:51:20.580
a better Soldier the suit allowed him to lift and&nbsp;
carry up to 1200 kilograms gave him stereoscopic&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:20.580 --> 01:51:26.520
vision and the organic radio transmitter allowed&nbsp;
him to communicate with other soldiers across long&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:26.520 --> 01:51:32.640
distances as well as listen in on encrypted enemy&nbsp;
Transmissions the wounds on his shoulders and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:32.640 --> 01:51:37.320
arms where it looked like appendages had been&nbsp;
ripped down were where his weapons and Supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:37.320 --> 01:51:42.480
packs had previously been mounted velotrov had&nbsp;
been stationed with his men in the area he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:42.480 --> 01:51:47.520
discovered when they were attacked by American&nbsp;
soldiers the soldiers had ripped off bellatrop's&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:47.520 --> 01:51:52.980
weapons and Supply pack and did the same to&nbsp;
all his men as well they were all shocked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:52.980 --> 01:51:58.500
belatrop being Bear for the moment only because&nbsp;
the Americans had identified him as the commanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:58.500 --> 01:52:04.860
officer suddenly there was a bright flash of light&nbsp;
and the American soldiers vanished not only that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:04.860 --> 01:52:10.440
but the landscape changed where there had been&nbsp;
a post-nuclear wasteland before now there were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:10.440 --> 01:52:16.800
lush green trees injured and disoriented belatrop&nbsp;
began sending distress signals only to have those&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:16.800 --> 01:52:23.340
signals intercepted by the SCP Foundation the most&nbsp;
interesting part of belatron's story as far as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:23.340 --> 01:52:28.620
foundation was concerned came during his fourth&nbsp;
interview when Dr Friedrich asked him about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:28.620 --> 01:52:33.780
engineers who had designed the exoskeleton&nbsp;
suit in belladrov's own words they weren't&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:33.780 --> 01:52:39.540
truly known to man until the years of the Great&nbsp;
War and the revolution the French found them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:39.540 --> 01:52:44.040
buried cities where the Western allies were&nbsp;
digging their trenches eventually they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:44.040 --> 01:52:50.340
made to build weapons for the war by both sides&nbsp;
by his description these Engineers didn't sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:50.340 --> 01:52:56.520
human but they were by no means unknown to&nbsp;
the SC EP Foundation bellatrov described the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:56.520 --> 01:53:02.280
engineers as humanoid nocturnal covered in fur&nbsp;
and possessing technology and intelligence far&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:02.280 --> 01:53:07.140
beyond that of any human those familiar with&nbsp;
the foundation's history might already know&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:07.140 --> 01:53:14.340
the engineers by their Foundation designation&nbsp;
of scp-1000 or their more commonly known name&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:14.340 --> 01:53:21.780
Bigfoot a quick rundown scp-1000 are a species of&nbsp;
intelligent humanoid primates that lived alongside&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:21.780 --> 01:53:27.180
Humanity in highly advanced cities until their&nbsp;
own technology was turned against them not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:27.180 --> 01:53:32.820
were there cities wiped off the face of the Earth&nbsp;
but 70 of their population was slaughtered and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:32.820 --> 01:53:37.980
survivors were driven mad to the point that their&nbsp;
mental faculties were no higher than those of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:37.980 --> 01:53:44.400
chimpanzee or gorilla the SCP Foundation is tasked&nbsp;
with keeping them away from Human contact in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:44.400 --> 01:53:49.740
fear of what the creatures would be able to do to&nbsp;
humanity if they ever regained their memories and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:49.740 --> 01:53:55.200
full mental function and rumors have been spread&nbsp;
about them possessing deadly anomalous properties&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:55.200 --> 01:54:01.380
but evidently the destruction of the scp-1000&nbsp;
species never occurred in bellatrop's universe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:01.380 --> 01:54:08.520
or if it did it wasn't nearly as complete in that&nbsp;
timeline Humanity regained contact with scp-1000&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:08.520 --> 01:54:13.740
after discovering their underground cities&nbsp;
sometime during World War one after this discovery&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:13.740 --> 01:54:19.320
both Allied and Axis Powers began recruiting them&nbsp;
to build weapons and their weapons played a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:19.320 --> 01:54:24.480
in both the Russian Revolution and the second&nbsp;
world war it was because of these technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:24.480 --> 01:54:30.480
advantages that the Cold War escalated into&nbsp;
what would become the war to end all wars both&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:30.480 --> 01:54:36.240
sides were armed with not only nuclear warheads&nbsp;
but highly Advanced radiation-proof exoskeleton&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:36.240 --> 01:54:41.520
armor that allowed the conflict to continue even&nbsp;
after the total Destruction of the planet while&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:41.520 --> 01:54:47.820
the humans fought above ground life continued as&nbsp;
normal for scp-1000 in their underground cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:47.820 --> 01:54:53.640
as velotrov noted they never wore the armor&nbsp;
themselves as they had no need to venture&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:53.640 --> 01:55:00.660
above ground only soldiers wear armor and this&nbsp;
is not their War bellatrov continued to adjust&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:00.660 --> 01:55:05.580
to life in containment and sitting for regular&nbsp;
interviews with Dr Friedrich he was given the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:05.580 --> 01:55:10.740
rations provided for humanoid anomalies but&nbsp;
in much larger quantities as the suit required&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:10.740 --> 01:55:16.380
him to eat around 8 000 calories a day to keep it&nbsp;
functioning bellatrov also began taking advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:16.380 --> 01:55:22.080
of his ability to access the foundation's library&nbsp;
he would often read or listen to classical music&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:22.080 --> 01:55:28.140
particularly by the Russian composer Tchaikovsky&nbsp;
he seemed especially fond of reading The Time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:28.140 --> 01:55:34.020
Machine by H.G Wells no surprise given he was able&nbsp;
to directly relate to the experience of living&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:34.020 --> 01:55:39.720
through an apocalypse and being transported&nbsp;
to a strange timeline with no way to return&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:39.720 --> 01:55:45.420
home but with little else to occupy his time&nbsp;
bellatrov began to display symptoms consistent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:45.420 --> 01:55:50.220
with post-traumatic stress disorder reporting&nbsp;
in his now regular interviews with Dr Friedrich&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:50.220 --> 01:55:55.320
that he was constantly reliving memories of the&nbsp;
battles he'd fought and the lives he had taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:55.320 --> 01:56:01.260
in combat the suit was making the problems worse&nbsp;
too it seemed it was as if he could never forget&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:01.260 --> 01:56:07.200
as one of its features was an ability to store&nbsp;
and replay memories this feature was intended&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:07.200 --> 01:56:12.120
as a strategic Aid that would allow commanding&nbsp;
officers to more effectively gather and process&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:12.120 --> 01:56:18.180
information about their surroundings but outside&nbsp;
of combat this only served to force balatrov into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:18.180 --> 01:56:24.900
reliving his most painful memories again and again&nbsp;
every night as he tried to sleep he would see the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:24.900 --> 01:56:31.200
faces of his men their weapons torn from their&nbsp;
exoskeletons laying in the dirt after being gunned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:31.200 --> 01:56:37.440
down by the enemy like many veterans bellatrov was&nbsp;
being hit all at once with the harsh realities of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:37.440 --> 01:56:43.260
War now that his brain wasn't solely focused on&nbsp;
keeping him alive it had time to process all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:43.260 --> 01:56:49.740
horrors he'd experienced he told Dr Friedrich I&nbsp;
trained alongside these men for years before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:49.740 --> 01:56:56.400
put us back on the surface since we were all young&nbsp;
children we brothers and I gave them the order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:56.400 --> 01:57:04.260
surrender and got them killed I should have died&nbsp;
with them while the story of scp-2273 ends here&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:04.260 --> 01:57:13.620
the story of Alexi bellatrov scp-2273-1 continues&nbsp;
in 2018 bellatrobe was allowed passage to volugrad&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:13.620 --> 01:57:18.720
Russia as part of an anomaly reintegration&nbsp;
program while he was unable to secure employment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:18.720 --> 01:57:23.760
within the Russian government as he had hoped&nbsp;
he eventually was taken in by a monastery and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:23.760 --> 01:57:28.860
continued to write letters to Dr Friedrich until&nbsp;
friedrich's eventual death from lymphoma-related&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:28.860 --> 01:57:34.260
complications there's plenty more on the&nbsp;
monastery the anomaly reintegration program and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:34.260 --> 01:57:39.180
what happened to Major belatrav when he returned&nbsp;
to Russia but that will have to be a story for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:39.180 --> 01:57:46.740
another day the SCP Foundation is an organization&nbsp;
that polices the abnormal in all its forms whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:46.740 --> 01:57:53.220
it's a multi-dimensional chaos God a big scaly&nbsp;
monster or a very effective coffee machine the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:53.220 --> 01:57:58.620
foundation takes appointed interest in pretty&nbsp;
much anything outside the norm so you're probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:58.620 --> 01:58:04.560
wondering what would they want with Mr Smith a&nbsp;
man who was about as normal and average as could&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:04.560 --> 01:58:11.280
be Mr Smith was a 47 year old African-American&nbsp;
construction worker with a wife and daughter he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:11.280 --> 01:58:17.220
lived a modest but contented life he had a close&nbsp;
circle of friends a happy family and was well&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:17.220 --> 01:58:23.400
liked among his co-workers but Mr Smith had no&nbsp;
idea about the anomalous nightmare barreling down&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:23.400 --> 01:58:30.360
the tracks towards him Mr Smith wasn't just about&nbsp;
to have his life destroyed by an anomaly he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:30.360 --> 01:58:35.580
about to become one it seemed like a normal day&nbsp;
at work on the construction site when he received&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:35.580 --> 01:58:41.160
a phone call it was the police informing him that&nbsp;
his wife and young daughter had been involved in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:41.160 --> 01:58:46.860
terrible car accident while this is anyone's worst&nbsp;
nightmare it was only the start of the horrifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:46.860 --> 01:58:53.580
things that were about to happen to poor poor Mr&nbsp;
Smith that night when Mr Smith went to sleep he&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:53.580 --> 01:58:59.340
fell victim to a strange phenomenon he started&nbsp;
to disappear while it would be easy to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:59.340 --> 01:59:04.860
off as the result of mental illness like a kind&nbsp;
of stressed-induced mental breakdown Mr Smith&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:04.860 --> 01:59:11.760
wasn't the only one who noticed or rather&nbsp;
didn't notice his co-workers would slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:11.760 --> 01:59:16.920
begin to forget details about him even obvious&nbsp;
things that you could glean from simply looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:16.920 --> 01:59:24.300
at a person like clothes height or skin color it&nbsp;
seemed that little by little ever since his family&nbsp;&nbsp;

